        <!-- seal ToString -->
        <section id="seal-tostring" class="section anchor hoverzoom">
          <div class="head">
            <div>11) Record types can seal <code>ToString()</code></div>
            <div class="tag">Type System</div>
          </div>
          <div class="body">
            <p>
              Records auto-generate a helpful <code>ToString()</code>. In inheritance scenarios, derived records could override it.
              C# 10 allows record authors to <span class="tt" data-tip="sealed prevents further overriding in derived types.\nIt locks a virtual member so subclasses cannot change it.\nUsed to protect behavior and improve predictability.">seal</span> <code>ToString()</code>
              to keep output consistent.
            </p>
            <p>
              This is valuable when logs, audit trails, or debugging tools depend on a stable string representation.
            </p>

            <div class="example">
              <div class="ex-title">ASCII: why seal matters</div>
              <pre>
Base record ToString() -> used in logs
     |
Derived record overrides ToString() -> logs change unexpectedly ❌

Seal ToString() -> derived cannot change it -> logs remain stable ✅
              </pre>
            </div>

            <div class="example">
              <div class="ex-title">Example: sealing ToString()</div>
              <pre>
public record Employee(string Name, int Id)
{
    public sealed override string ToString() =&gt; $"Emp({Id}) - {Name}";
}

public class Program
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        var e = new Employee("Ananya", 101);
        Console.WriteLine(e.ToString());
    }
}
              </pre>
              <a class="trybtn" href="#!" title="Dummy link">Try It Yourself</a>
            </div>

            <p><b>Real-world use case:</b> Audit systems where consistent ToString() output is used in logs or compliance reports.</p>

            <div class="tip">
              <b>Beginner Tip:</b> If you don’t use inheritance, you may not need sealing. But it’s good to know for large systems.
            </div>
            <div class="warning">
              <b>Advanced Note:</b> Sealing is a design decision—use it when you must preserve behavior for derived types.
            </div>

